      "lineNbr": 267,
      "author": {
        "id": 1011323
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-22T11:26:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Confirmed in the built HTML: every description here currently renders as a monospace `\u003cpre\u003e` literal block (4 in the output), and the `1.`–`6.` points render as their own list instead of nesting under \"The Vulnerability\".\n\nI built this replacement with the doc pipeline and verified it — 0 literal blocks, a proper description list with the six points as a nested numbered list, all prose as paragraphs:\n\n```\nConfidentiality::\nThe vulnerability details must not be shared with anyone outside the\nimmediate team reporting the issue and the Gerrit Maintainers. No data\nrelated to this vulnerability has been entered into external tools,\npublic trackers, or AI-assisted analyzers.\n\nThe Vulnerability::\n+\n. *Expected Behavior*\n+\nWhat is the intended behavior? Please link to the relevant evidence in\nthe Gerrit documentation if present.\n. *Observed Behavior*\n+\nWhat actually happens? (Note: pure code analysis without execution\nevidence is not accepted.)\n. *Reproduction \u0026 Environment*\n+\n--\n* *Affected Versions:* list all impacted non-EOL versions and whether the\n  issue is present on the master branch.\n* *Configuration Triggers:* does this require specific options to be set,\n  or does it happen on a default Gerrit installation?\n--\n. *Steps to Reproduce*\n+\nProvide an end-to-end walkthrough covering all impacted endpoints: SSH,\nREST-API, Git protocol, etc. (Note: pure code analysis without execution\nsteps is not accepted.)\n. *Security Impact*\n+\n--\n* *Data Exposure \u0026 Access:* does this leak sensitive information or bypass\n  access protections?\n* *Data Integrity:* can an attacker compromise, modify, or destroy private\n  data?\n--\n. *Validation \u0026 Mitigation*\n+\n--\n* *Admin Validation:* how can a Gerrit admin verify if their installation\n  is vulnerable? (e.g., a script, curl command, or log check)\n* *Proposed Mitigation:* are there immediate steps admins can take to\n  block the exploit without upgrading? (e.g., reverse proxy rules,\n  disabling features)\n--\n```",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 227,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 267,
